7592186100857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96c23e89-1183-51f8-96e1-860b01688bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db25438-048c-53f0-8355-741e5e2ebec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572352} {:answer/id #uuid "4a8739b8-ed92-5364-9faa-5ba91c071e38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572406} {:answer/id #uuid "92364a35-2247-55e6-840a-29fd59670617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572473} {:answer/id #uuid "c2999aba-6d6b-5e91-9a5a-fc3590d5a58e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572534} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bac031-36ca-5e9b-ba04-39667e3deff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3bac031-36ca-5e9b-ba04-39667e3deff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572582} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a3e14fd-5ecf-500c-a39a-19355d43800b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a7ee813-eddb-55cf-91b1-fd20b615ae7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-18652" :question/problem "![](images/36/3699487cf2de2ca4e996fb57f46e167495c8a118a4b4a5421e665e2bb7f84eff.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,4]$ given that $f(-3)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of $2$ trapezoids. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/8a/8a7521b862c6533554df3931b03f7aa4b6b669167ced130e4bb353605fd24ea2.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =4 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-4+6 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{4,0,6\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5bcc62c-f716-503f-9195-b1f8f1c78e04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af254440-71f6-5905-bac5-63630bc751b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af254440-71f6-5905-bac5-63630bc751b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572507} :answer-field/id #uuid "20bcace7-b79d-5f62-8513-7256f1de024e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b47a3384-43e3-5364-9f00-98abf9eb16af" :question/math-academy-id "q-316970" :question/problem "![](images/99/994e49d2360232104932da5a7798e1621071676ccad9015908303aafc36c7258.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,4].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $3$ and $5$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-2,4]$ given that $g(-2)=1.$\n\n${g}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $g(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,4),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,1,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-2) & =1 \\\\ g(1) & =g(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ g(4) & =g(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{4}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+(3-5) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $g$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{1,4,-1\\}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5bcc62c-f716-503f-9195-b1f8f1c78e04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eaa4478-2a46-5b09-9364-94441e0b00a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2eaa4478-2a46-5b09-9364-94441e0b00a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572371} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e546688-2936-58e2-b8d3-3e58a35fd2d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c20cc6cd-9570-529a-a16b-801a9c33c8af" :question/math-academy-id "q-343709" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3fcbed98f7838b4885e2ca14cb1192c039e0b1d35e5980cca363ec4cdc6f9f5.png)\n\nThe function $w(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,3].$ The graph of ${w}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $w(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $7,$ $5,$ and $1$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $w(x)$ on the interval $[-6,3]$ given that $w(-6)=-4.$\n\n${w}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $w(x)$ on $[-6,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${w}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-6,3),$ and ${w}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2,1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,-2,1,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $w$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w(-6) & =-4 \\\\ w(-2) & =w(-6)+{\\int }_{-6}^{-2}{w}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4+7 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ w(1) & =w(-6)+{\\int }_{-6}^{1}{w}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4+(7-5) \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ w(3) & =w(-6)+{\\int }_{-6}^{3}{w}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4+(7-5+1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $w$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $w$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{w}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-4,3,-2,-1\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $w$ is ${w}_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $w(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96c23e89-1183-51f8-96e1-860b01688bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb3aaf9-45e4-5646-991a-a616558b85aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eb3aaf9-45e4-5646-991a-a616558b85aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572356} :answer-field/id #uuid "4671f731-8e23-5f03-b18a-1bc829f830e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63b0fa41-9cba-5e63-9815-21bb52243039" :question/math-academy-id "q-343898" :question/problem "![](images/15/15b37063b57cc846bfc1776c1d6ec50a2170440289da1e7482b365c197c11410.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ if $f(-3)=10.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,1,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of a trapezoid and a triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/bd/bd1d4466a6a0d7f8640200c999170dff2aa2762fc2cd14a8e61c110c6e94b189.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =10 \\\\ f(1) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10-5 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10+(-5+2) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{10,5,7\\}=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=10,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-3.$"}]}
